## 9. Planned migration: dedicated Hetzner project (`psmdb-bazel-rbe`)

> Status: planned. New Hetzner project already created (id 14460169).
> Cutover sequence below is to be executed AFTER PSMDB-2055 v8.3 build is
> green on the current shared project and the matching cherry-picks are
> merged across `master`, `v8.3`, and `v8.0`.

### 9.1 Why split

- Hetzner Cloud API rate-limit is **per-project, default 3600 req/hr**,
  refilling at 1 req/sec
  (https://docs.hetzner.cloud/reference/cloud#description/rate-limiting).
- The current shared project hosts 6 Jenkins instances (psmdb +
  5 others) plus the BuildBarn control plane (scaler, ondemand workers,
  central preflight). Observed quota drain to **2 / 3600 within seconds**
  during multi-stage parallel builds — once exhausted, the next
  `hcloud server create` fails for ~tens of minutes regardless of which
  team triggered the burst. Cross-team blast radius.
- Volume quota is **also per-project** and currently 850 / 1024 GB used
  in the shared project, which makes any in-place rsync-based volume
  migration impossible (no headroom for a parallel 750 GB volume).

Splitting BB into its own project gives us:
- Independent 3600 req/hr budget — net effective ~2× capacity.
- Independent volume quota — full 1024 GB available for CAS growth.
- Operational isolation — bursts from any of the 5 unrelated Jenkins
  instances no longer starve the BB control plane, and vice versa.

### 9.2 Hetzner constraints (sanity-checked before planning)

| Asset | Cross-project? | Migration path |
|---|---|---|
| Project | n/a | New project `psmdb-bazel-rbe` (id 14460169) created. |
| API token | per-project | Generate fresh in the new project. |
| SSH keys | per-project | Re-upload public keys under the same names. |
| Private network | per-project | Create new network in the new project (CIDR may stay `10.0.0.0/16`; nothing connects across). |
| Floating IP | **transfer-to-project (UI flag)** | Migrate the OLD FIP across — preserves the IP literal that DNS already points at, so `bb-psmdb.ddns.net` A-record needs zero changes (no TTL outage). Pre-condition: FIP must be unassigned (`hcloud floating-ip unassign`). UI-only as of 2026-05; `hcloud` CLI has no equivalent. `create-central.sh::ensure_floating_ip` re-assigns it at the API level, then `configure_floating_ip_on_host` aliases it onto the VM's eth0 (`ip addr add` + persistent `/etc/network/interfaces.d/60-floating-ip`). The OS-level alias is mandatory: Hetzner FIPs are NOT L2-routed to the assigned VM, so the kernel rejects packets addressed to a FIP that isn't configured locally. |
| Volume (xfs CAS, 750 GB) | **transfer-to-project (UI flag)** | Warm CAS migration — detach from old project, "Transfer to project" → `psmdb-bazel-rbe`. See §9.3. Volume keeps its numeric ID and on-disk xfs after transfer; `create-central.sh MODE=attach` mounts it read-as-is. |
| Server | per-project | Spin up fresh `bb-psmdb-ondemand` in new project. |
| TLS cert | DNS-bound | Re-issue Let's Encrypt cert on the new central before DNS cutover. |
| DNS hostname (`bb-psmdb.ddns.net`) | external (No-IP) | Single A-record swap to new FIP — Bazel/CI configs untouched. |

### 9.3 Volume strategy: warm transfer

**Verified 2026-05-06**: Hetzner's "Transfer to project" UI action moves
a detached Cloud Volume between projects in-place. The volume retains
its numeric ID, xfs filesystem, and on-disk contents; only the project
ownership changes. (No equivalent in `hcloud` CLI as of this writing —
console-only.) Test executed with a 10 GB scratch volume from
`ps3.cd.htz` → `psmdb-bazel-rbe` and back, both directions confirmed.

This obsoletes the original cold-rebuild plan. New procedure:

1. Drain old central: stop containers, unmount `/var/lib/buildbarn`,
   detach the volume from `bb-psmdb-ondemand` in the old project
   (`hcloud volume detach 105484418`).
2. In Hetzner Console → old project → Volumes → `psmdb-buildbarn-
   cas-ondemand` → ⋮ menu → **"Transfer to project"** → select
   `psmdb-bazel-rbe`. Confirm. (Few-second operation.)
3. The volume now appears in `psmdb-bazel-rbe`, still
   `psmdb-buildbarn-cas-ondemand`, still ID 105484418, still xfs with
   warm CAS data intact.
4. Run `create-central.sh` with `MODE=attach` — `mount_volume` detects
   xfs and mounts as-is; `detect_state` returns `populated`; the
   attach branch refreshes configs without touching CAS data.

Net effect: zero CAS warm-up time post-cutover. First Bazel build sees
the same cache it saw before cutover (60-90 % hit rate band), instead
of paying ~3 h to repopulate.

Trade-off: brief outage window (~5 min for detach + transfer + reattach
+ mount + container start) replaces the ~3 h cold-cache penalty.

### 9.4 Cutover sequence (operator runbook)

Pre-conditions:
- PSMDB-2055 v8.3 build green on the shared-project central.
- PSMDB-2034 / PSMDB-2054 cherry-picks merged across `master`, `v8.3`, `v8.0`.

**Phase 1 — pre-create resources in new project (no impact on production)**

1. In `psmdb-bazel-rbe` (id 14460169):
   - Create Hetzner API token; store under
     `~/.config/psmdb-rbe/hcloud.token` (mode `0600`).
   - Upload SSH public key `bb-psmdb-ssh` (operator's pubkey).
     `bb-psmdb-ondemand-central` (worker-fleet key) is generated and
     uploaded automatically by `create-central.sh::ensure_central_ssh_key()`.
   - Create private network `psmdb-bazel-rbe` (CIDR `10.30.246.0/24` —
     `/24` is fine, the 100-resource network ceiling is the binding
     constraint, not address space).

     **AND immediately add a Subnet that covers the FULL network
     range, not the Hetzner UI's `/28` default**. Two pitfalls
     conflated here, both burned us during PSMDB-2034:

     - A subnet-less Network exists but server creation fails at
       scaler runtime with `no_subnet_available`, in every region,
       every spawn.
     - A `/28` subnet (Hetzner UI default) gives ~11 useable IPs
       — fine for one central + a few workers, but matrix-load
       (`max_total_nodes=72`) drains it mid-build and the failure
       trickles in as `no_subnet_available` on slow-spawning
       pools, masking the root cause.

     Use the network's own `/24`:

     ```bash
     hcloud network add-subnet ${HCLOUD_NETWORK_ID} \
         --type cloud \
         --network-zone eu-central \
         --ip-range 10.30.246.0/24
     ```

     If a `/28` already exists (UI mis-default) and the central is
     live, you cannot resize in place — Hetzner has no subnet-edit
     API. Either:

     - **No-downtime**: add a non-overlapping second subnet
       (`10.30.246.128/25` → +124 useable IPs); Hetzner will pick
       free IPs from any subnet of the same `network-zone` at
       server-create time.
     - **Clean**: schedule downtime, `hcloud server detach-from-network`
       the central, `hcloud network delete-subnet`, re-add `/24`,
       re-attach.

     `create-central.sh::preflight()` validates BOTH `subnet_count
     ≥ 1` AND `useable_total ≥ 1.5 × max_total_nodes` before
     proceeding — fail-fast with the exact `add-subnet` line.
   - **Do NOT allocate a fresh Floating IP** — we transfer the old
     project's FIP across in Phase 3 so DNS doesn't have to change
     (see §9.2 table). If a spare FIP was created earlier, delete it
     before migration: `hcloud floating-ip delete <name>`.
2. Capture the resource IDs: network, ssh-key. Volume ID and Floating
   IP ID stay the same as they do in the OLD project (UI transfer
   preserves them — see Phase 3).

**Phase 2 — secrets escrow (ON THE OLD CENTRAL, BEFORE DRAIN)**

3. Snapshot the live `.env` from the old central; this carries the
   `BB_PORTAL_DB_PASSWORD` (must match the Postgres PGDATA on the volume,
   which travels with the transfer) and the OIDC client secrets / cookie
   seeds (regen at cutover would log every operator out):

   ```bash
   ssh -i ~/.ssh/htz.cd.bb-psmdb-ondemand.key root@bb-psmdb.ddns.net \
       'cat /var/lib/buildbarn/compose/.env' \
     > ~/.config/psmdb-rbe/old-central.env
   chmod 600 ~/.config/psmdb-rbe/old-central.env
   ```

4. Create operator-local `~/.config/psmdb-rbe/cluster.env`
   (template in §10.2). Make sure it exports:
   - `HCLOUD_TOKEN_FILE` → new project's token
   - `NETWORK_ID`, `SSH_KEY_IDS`, `FLOATING_IP_ID` → IDs from Phase 1
   - `VOLUME_ID` → keeps the OLD project's numeric ID (transfer preserves)
   - `MODE=attach` → tells `create-central.sh` to mount the populated volume
     in-place (no `mkfs.xfs`)
   - `PRESERVE_ENV_FROM=$HOME/.config/psmdb-rbe/old-central.env` → tells
     `create-central.sh` to seed `.env` on the new central from the old
     one before `bake_env` runs, preserving the Postgres + OIDC secrets
   - `GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID` / `_SECRET` → reuse from old App
     (callback URL `https://bb-psmdb.ddns.net:5556/callback` survives
     the cutover unchanged)

**Phase 3 — drain + warm transfer (THIS IS THE OUTAGE WINDOW)**

5. Drain the old central:
   ```bash
   ssh root@bb-psmdb.ddns.net 'cd /var/lib/buildbarn/compose && docker compose down && umount /var/lib/buildbarn'
   ```
6. In the OLD project, detach the volume and unassign the FIP:
   ```bash
   hcloud context use ps3.cd.htz
   hcloud volume detach 105484418
   hcloud floating-ip unassign <OLD-FIP-ID>      # makes "Transfer to project" clickable in UI
   ```
7. In Hetzner Console → old project, run the UI transfer twice:
   - Volumes → `psmdb-buildbarn-cas-ondemand` → ⋮ → **"Transfer to
     project"** → `psmdb-bazel-rbe`. Confirm.
   - Floating IPs → `bb-psmdb` → ⋮ → **"Transfer to project"** →
     `psmdb-bazel-rbe`. Confirm.

   Both operations are few-second, console-only as of 2026-05;
   `hcloud` CLI has no equivalent. Numeric IDs and IP literals are
   preserved across the transfer, so cluster.env values that reference
   them stay valid.

**Phase 4 — bring up new central**

8. Run the script with the patched / preserved-secrets flow:
   ```bash
   source ~/.config/psmdb-rbe/cluster.env
   cd jenkins-pipelines/IaC/buildbarn/ondemand/scripts
   ./create-central.sh
   ```
   The script will, in order:
   - preflight (validate IDs, OAuth creds, `PRESERVE_ENV_FROM` mode/format)
   - `ensure_server` — create `bb-psmdb-ondemand` (cpx62, Debian 13)
   - `attach_volume` — re-attach the just-transferred volume
   - `ensure_floating_ip` — assign the just-transferred FIP to the new
     server (FIP arrives in new project unassigned, so this is a plain
     "assign", not a cutover from another VM). DNS A-record for
     `bb-psmdb.ddns.net` is unchanged because the IP literal didn't
     change — only the project that owns it.
   - `wait_for_ssh` / `bootstrap_host` / `mount_volume` — Docker, xfs mount
   - `detect_state` → `populated`; `prompt_mode` honours preset `MODE=attach`
   - `sync_configs` — rsync compose/, then **pre-seed** `.env` from
     `PRESERVE_ENV_FROM` so `bake_env::_env_get` preserves every secret
   - `bake_env` — render configs with the preserved secrets
   - `compose_up` — bring up containers; bb-portal-db consumes the same
     Postgres password the PGDATA on the volume was initialised with → no
     "auth failed" loop
   - smoke tests, summary
9. DNS: nothing to do — same FIP, same IP literal, same A-record.
10. Smoke from a workstation: `bazel build --config=psmdb_buildfarm ...`
    against `bb-psmdb.ddns.net:8981`. First action lookup should hit
    the warm CAS immediately (no minute-long cold-cache penalty).
11. Trigger a full Jenkins matrix build on PSMDB-2055 v8.3 to validate
    end-to-end.

**Phase 5 — cool-down**

12. Keep the OLD `bb-psmdb-ondemand` powered off for 2 weeks as
    rollback insurance. The old volume is gone (transferred), but the
    VM itself is intact and can re-mount a fresh volume if we ever
    need to roll back to a cold-cache state on the shared project.
13. After 2 weeks of stable operation: delete old VM in shared project.

### 9.5 Refactor as separate Git repo (follow-up)

Once §9.4 is stable, the natural next step is to extract
`IaC/buildbarn/` into its own repository (working name
`psmdb-buildbarn` or `percona-buildbarn`):

- One repo = one Hetzner project = one operational unit.
- Lookup-by-name pattern from §10 already supports it (no hardcoded IDs
  to port).
- `jenkins-pipelines` keeps only the Jenkins Groovy / YAML; the BB
  cluster gets its own versioning, its own CI (env validation,
  jsonnet linting), its own release tags.

Out of scope for the migration ticket itself — track separately.

## 10. Operator-local cluster config (lookup-by-name pattern)

Hetzner numeric IDs (`VOLUME_ID`, `NETWORK_ID`, `SSH_KEY_IDS`) are
**project-local** and not safe to commit. Replaced by name-based
lookup + operator-local override file. Names ARE safe to commit
(they're stable and human-readable; the IDs they resolve to live in
the active hcloud auth context).

### 10.1 Repo-side: `create-central.sh` resolution order

```
For each of VOLUME_ID / NETWORK_ID / SSH_KEY_IDS:
  1. If the env var is already set (from cluster.env or a one-shot
     export), use it as-is.
  2. Otherwise: hcloud describe by name (VOLUME_NAME / NETWORK_NAME
     / each entry in SSH_KEY_NAMES) within the currently-authenticated
     Hetzner project. Fail loudly if anything fails to resolve.

Names committed in script:
  VOLUME_NAME    = psmdb-buildbarn-cas-ondemand
  NETWORK_NAME   = psmdb-bazel-rbe
  SSH_KEY_NAMES  = bb-psmdb-laptop bb-psmdb-ondemand-central
```

This makes the script project-portable: same code, same names, the
hcloud token determines which project's IDs are picked up.

### 10.2 Operator-side: `~/.config/psmdb-rbe/cluster.env`

Operator-local, never committed. Sourced before each
`create-central.sh` run:

```
source ~/.config/psmdb-rbe/cluster.env
cd jenkins-pipelines/IaC/buildbarn/ondemand/scripts
./create-central.sh
```

Template:

```bash
# psmdb-bazel-rbe (Hetzner project 14460169) — operator-local config.
# NEVER commit. NEVER share via Slack/email.

# ---------- Hetzner auth (pick ONE) ----------
export HCLOUD_TOKEN_FILE="$HOME/.config/psmdb-rbe/hcloud.token"  # preferred (file mode 0600)
# export HCLOUD_CONTEXT_NAME=psmdb-bazel-rbe                      # alt: hcloud cli.toml context
# export HCLOUD_TOKEN="..."                                        # least preferred (env leaks)

# ---------- Hetzner resource IDs (project-local, look up by name once) ----------
# hcloud network describe psmdb-bazel-rbe   -o format='{{.ID}}'
# hcloud ssh-key describe bb-psmdb-ssh      -o format='{{.ID}}'
# hcloud floating-ip describe bb-psmdb-central -o format='{{.ID}}'
export NETWORK_ID=12197477                  # psmdb-bazel-rbe (10.30.246.0/24)
export SSH_KEY_IDS="111911127"              # bb-psmdb-ssh (operator's pubkey)
export FLOATING_IP_ID=129643630             # bb-psmdb-central → 95.217.242.120
export VOLUME_ID=105484418                  # CAS volume — same numeric ID after cross-project transfer

# ---------- Server config ----------
export SERVER_NAME=bb-psmdb-ondemand
export SERVER_TYPE=cpx62                    # 16 vCPU AMD / 32 GB RAM (was cpx42 default)
export LOCATION=hel1
export OS_IMAGE=debian-13

# ---------- Local SSH private key (matches Hetzner SSH key bb-psmdb-ssh) ----------
export SSH_PRIV_KEY="$HOME/.ssh/bb-psmdb-ssh.key"

# ---------- Public network identity ----------
export PUBLIC_HOSTNAME=bb-psmdb.ddns.net
export LE_EMAIL=builds-noreply@percona.com

# ---------- Volume mode ----------
# 'attach' — volume already has CAS data after cross-project transfer;
# create-central.sh::mount_volume mounts as-is, no mkfs.xfs.
export MODE=attach

# ---------- Warm-migration secrets escrow ----------
# Path to a snapshot of the OLD central's /var/lib/buildbarn/compose/.env.
# create-central.sh rsyncs this onto the new central before bake_env runs,
# so _env_get preserves BB_PORTAL_DB_PASSWORD (must match the Postgres
# PGDATA on the migrated volume) and the OIDC/cookie secrets (regen
# would log every operator out at cutover). Capture with:
#
#   ssh root@<old-central> 'cat /var/lib/buildbarn/compose/.env' \
#     > ~/.config/psmdb-rbe/old-central.env
#   chmod 600 ~/.config/psmdb-rbe/old-central.env
#
# Mandatory for warm-CAS migration; leave empty for fresh-stack deployments.
export PRESERVE_ENV_FROM="$HOME/.config/psmdb-rbe/old-central.env"

# ---------- GitHub OAuth App (Dex GitHub connector) ----------
# Reuse from old central — callback URL https://bb-psmdb.ddns.net:5556/callback
# survives the cutover, so the same App keeps working.
# Extract from PRESERVE_ENV_FROM after the snapshot above:
export GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID="$(grep '^GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=' "$PRESERVE_ENV_FROM" | cut -d= -f2-)"
export GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET="$(grep '^GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=' "$PRESERVE_ENV_FROM" | cut -d= -f2-)"
```

Recommended permissions:

```
chmod 700 ~/.config/psmdb-rbe
chmod 600 ~/.config/psmdb-rbe/hcloud.token
chmod 600 ~/.config/psmdb-rbe/cluster.env
```

## 11. Known capacity / rate-limit observations

Documented evidence supporting §9 split decision and adjacent backlog
items. Update when new data points arrive.

### 11.1 aarch64 stage start latency in PSMDB-2055 builds

Long aarch64 stage warm-up times (5–20 min before the worker starts
executing actions) traced to two compounding factors:
- **Hetzner API rate-limit pressure** — aarch64 worker spawn requests
  competed with polling traffic from other Jenkins instances in the
  shared project; remaining quota dropped low enough that
  `hcloud server create` calls were briefly stalled.
- **Hetzner aarch64 capacity transient unavailability in `hel1`** —
  several spawn attempts returned "no capacity" until Hetzner freed
  the requested type, independent of our quota.

Both worsen under shared rate-limit; expected to soften noticeably
after §9 split (own quota) but not fully resolved
(aarch64 capacity is upstream of any quota fix).

### 11.2 Central host memory pressure

Snapshots taken under load:
- 15 GiB physical RAM, 93 % utilized at idle.
- 16.1 GiB peak under PSMDB-2055 multi-stage parallel build (swap
  involvement; envoy-proxy observed swapping briefly).
- `storage-0` / `storage-1` (bb-storage shards) consume the largest
  slice — both keep CAS index in mmap'd memory.

Justification basis for a future host bump-up. Track separately —
not blocking the project split.

### 11.3 Envoy circuit-breaker headroom

After §"PSMDB-2034 envoy: lift circuit breakers" patch (max_connections
/ max_pending_requests / max_requests / max_retries → 100 000), all
overflow counters stay at zero under 17 parallel Jenkins stages with
`--jobs=72` each. Verified via `:9901/stats?filter=overflow`.

Notable surprise during observation: `cluster.dex_jwks.upstream_cx_active`
held at ~263 connections — far higher than expected from infrequent
JWKS rotation. Worth a follow-up to confirm Envoy isn't leaking
short-lived JWKS connections, but no functional impact.

